{"id":"cpc-outcomes-2022-2026","title":"Planning and Historic Preservation Commissions, August 2022 to July 2026: approved, changed, refused or put off","question":"What did the two commissions do with the items before them after August 2022, and is it different from 2019-2022?","finding":"The 210 hearings in the minutes saved for 2022-08-17 to 2026-07-09 record 1,195 decisions and 476 continuances (27.8% of actions, against 35.8% in 2019-01 to 2022-08). 11 decisions were disapprovals (0.9%, against 2.3% before) and 209 approvals with changes; the share changed or disapproved is 18.4% against 27.4% before. By year the share continued runs from 16.2% to 31.5%.","how":"Each saved minutes file was turned to text (pdftotext) and read item by item: one row per ACTION line. The action is sorted by its words with the same rules as the 2019-2022 table (the “votes” analysis): continued; withdrawn; disapproved; approved with conditions; approved as amended, with modifications or after taking discretionary review (approved with changes); and plain approvals and adoptions. Informational items, \"reviewed and commented\" and the Zoning Administrator's own rulings at joint hearings are left out. The 2019-2022 rows are the earlier parsed table on disk, from January 2019.","limits":"Outright refusal is rare because a weak proposal is usually withdrawn or reshaped before a vote. A continuance is often asked for by the applicant, or agreed with neighbors, and one project with several approvals is counted once per approval. The State housing laws passed in these years removed many projects from hearings altogether, so the mix of items changed; the comparison between periods is of what came before the commissions, not of all projects. One file (the “20251001 hpc min” text) holds no numbered items and for 4 January 2023 the archive links only a hearing notice, not minutes.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"period","t":"Period","type":"text"},{"k":"group","t":"Breakdown","type":"text"},